{title: Softly Now the Light of Day}
{artist: George W. Doane / Carl Maria von Weber}
{genre: Hymn}
{style: Traditional}
{flow: Closing}
{key: F}
{tempo: 108}
{time: 4/4}
{c: Chords checked against the four-part harmony of the Cyber Hymnal score (The Hymnbook (Richmond, Virginia: Presbyterian Church in the United States, 1955), # 60. This is a theme from Weber\'s last opera, Oberon; Henry W. Greatorex ar)}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[F]Softly now the light of [C]day
[F]Fades [Dm]u[A]pon [D7]my [Gm]sight [C7]a[F]way;
[F]Free from care, from labor [C]free,
[F]Lord, [D7]I [Gm]would com[C]mune with [F]Thee.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[F]Thou, whose all pervading [C]eye
[F]Naught [Dm]es[A]capes, [D7]with[Gm]out, [C7]with[F]in,
[F]Pardon each infirmi[C]ty,
[F]O[D7]pen [Gm]fault, and [C]secret [F]sin.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[F]Soon for me the light of [C]day
[F]Shall [Dm]for[A]ev[D7]er [Gm]pass [C7]a[F]way;
[F]Then, from sin and sorrow [C]free,
[F]Take [D7]me, [Gm]Lord, to [C]dwell with [F]Thee.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[F]Thou who, sinless, yet hast [C]known
[F]All [Dm]of [A]man’s [D7]in[Gm]fir[C7]mi[F]ty;
[F]Then, from Thine eternal throne,
[C]Je[F]sus, [D7]look [Gm]with pi[C]tying [F]eye.
{end_of_verse}
